Documentation for PRUN12.COM			03/28/84


Description:

PRUN is an independent command run facility for CP/M and ZCPR2 which allows
execution of PRL files.

PRUN is compatible with CP/M 2.x with either stock CCP or ZCPR.  PRUN can be
implemented as "extended command facility" under ZCPR2.  It will, however,
not honor search paths.  The PRL file to be run MUST exist on the currently
logged drive or else execution will fail. As of version 1.20, PRUN will now
correctly parse the command line and place the two FCBs at 5C and 6C. Also,
if the PRL file is not found, PRUN will simply echo "FILENAME?" and return
to CP/M. If there are no command errors and the PRL file is not corrupt,
an unsuspecting use will be unable to see any difference between CCP and PRUN.


PRL FILES:
==========
PRL files are defined in the MP/M manual.  This is a brief explanation:

PRL files consist of 3 parts, a header, a code block and a bit map.
The header is 256 bytes long and defined as follows:
BYTE 0		Undocumented. Usually 00, could be FDH
BYTE 1 & 2	The size of the code block in bytes
BYTE 3		Undefined
BYTE 4 & 5	Buffer space required beyond the end of program
BYTE 6 to 255	RESERVED
After the header, the code, ORGed at 100H, follows. It is followed by
a bit map, where each bit describes one byte in the program. If the
bit is SET, the corresponding byte is relocatable, else it is absolute.

Digital Research's LINK will correctly produce OGR-100 PRL files with
the [OP] option. Do not use the [OS] option or any [Lnnnn] option.


Requirements:

PRUN will execute properly in any Z80 CP/M 2.2 system.  PRUN will not
execute in 8080 systems.  In mode 0, PRUN is unnecessary in CP/M plus,
as that operating system honors the PRL protocol.


Operation:

PRUN in mode 0 will correctly place a standard PRL file at
0100H and attempt execution.  The PRL file must be ORGed at 0100H.

To generate an org-zero PRL file with DRI's LINK, use the following
LINK option immediately after the PRL file name: [OP,L0], i.e.:
A>LINK PRUN[OP,L0]=PRUN,SYSLIB[S]

Two additional modes of operation are available:

1. Relocate to below BDOS and run
2. Relocate to specified address and run

MODE 1:
=======

Under mode 1, the PRL file is relocated so that it's last byte is
located in the page immediately preceeding BDOS and then execution is
attempted at the program's start address.  No provisions are made, however
to protect the program from overwriting.  This is the sole responsibility
of the program itself.

Under mode 2, the PRL file is relocated to a specific user-supplied
address and executed.  PRUN will do no range checking and allow the file
to be relocated to anywhere in RAM, except 0000H or at a location which
would cause part of the program to wrap around at FFFFH.

HOW TO IMPLEMENT PRUN MODES:
============================

Basic operation (mode 0) requires no special processing of the PRL file,
except that it MUST be orged at 0.

Modes 1 and 2 require a "relocation control block" (RCB) to be located
immediately following the program.  The RCB is always 5 bytes long.

BYTE 1 : 76H	(always)
BYTE 2 : 76H	(always)
BYTE 3 : nn	If nn=0 THEN mode 1, else nn= starting page of relocated
		program in mode 2.
BYTE 4 and 5	Program entry point

It is very unlikely that any type executable 8080 or Z80 file will ever
end with two HLT instructions, so it is safe to assume that 7676H
indicates the presence of an RCB.  (This idea borrowed from
SQ/USQ, where 76FF is used at the start of a squeezed file.)

The RCB has to be added at the end of the ASM file, immediately before
the END statement.  Example:

.... code ....
	ds	50
stack	equ	$	;last regular program line
;
	db	76h	;RCB id#1
	db	76h	;RCB id#2
	db	0	;select mode 1
	dw	begin	;entry point (usually first byte of code)
	end



